<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fakhruddin Mubarak Shah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1338–<a href="/years/1349">1349</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fakhruddin Mubarak Shah was the founder of the Sonargaon Sultanate in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. An armour-bearer of the Tughluq governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonargaon">Sonargaon</a>, he seized power on his master's death in 1338 and declared independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a>, the first ruler in Bengal to break from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. He struck his own coins and campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a>. The traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited his realm.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fakhruddin-mubarak-shah.jpg" alt="Fakhruddin Mubarak Shah" width="320" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Faysalzillur8 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sonargaon_Upazila_in_narayanganj_Zila.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
